WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:07.610
So hi, my name is
Harvey Lieberman,

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:11.000
and I am here today to tell
you a little bit about the, R

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.840
pharma organization,
who we are, what we do,

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.860
and how you can get involved.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.710
If you're interested.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:24.810
So R is ubiquitous within
the pharmaceutical industry.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.820
But until recently,
there hasn't really

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:30.180
been a group that works across
multiple pharma companies

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:34.410
to provide a space
for users to interact.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:39.420
In 2017, a group of R users
from several pharma companies

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.430
got together with
other organizations

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:45.600
interested in this space,
such as the FDA and Harvard,

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:46.860
to form a group.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.180
And that's how R
pharma was born.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:56.120
R/Pharma is an R
Consortium ISC working group,

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.340
it's organized by a
group of volunteers

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:01.940
from multiple organizations and
some of the companies involved

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.760
in setting up the
R/pharma conference

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:05.845
are listed on this slide.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.780
Now, the goal of
R/pharma has been

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:18.430
to host a scientifically
and industry oriented

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:21.010
conference focused
on the use of R

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:24.160
in the development of
pharmaceuticals. The conference

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.780
covers topics such as genomics,
drug discovery, pk, pd,

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:32.920
clinical trials, regulatory
compliance and validation,

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:36.430
as well as more general topics
such as reproducible research

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.940
and DevOps.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:41.730
The focus is on the use
of R as a primary,

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:45.300
but not necessarily exclusive
tool in the drug development

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.330
process.

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:50.630
We also hold workshops
at the conference,

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:54.560
but since the primary focus
is for R practitioners,

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.050
the workshops are
really aimed at those

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.540
who are already
competent in R.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.470
Anyone's welcome to
present a workshop.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.140
And we we're lucky to have
some really amazing presenters,

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.890
as you'll see on some
of the later slides.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:14.180
Now there are plenty of data
science conferences, workshops

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:16.220
and even organizations.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:19.450
So what makes us different?

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:23.730
Well, for one, we're run
by pharma, for pharma.

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:26.100
We concentrate on
R, which is not

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.890
to say we don't have
contributing talks that include

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:34.870
python, Sas or even julia, but
just that R is the main focus.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:39.640
The R/pharma conference that
we've held the past three years

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:42.290
has been free.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.710
Any cost has been assumed
by an advisory group, which

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:49.060
is generally made up of pharma
companies willing to support

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:51.610
the cross-fertilisation
that occurs

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:53.890
when you put a bunch
of curious programmers

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:58.930
together. In a similar
vein, we have no sponsors

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:04.030
and nor do we allow sponsorship
at the conference or any events

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:08.290
because we believe the community
led effort works best when

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:11.110
there's no outside influence.

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.560
We're also focused on
diversity and have

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.590
goals on providing travel grants
to those who need assistance

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:19.750
in attending the meetings.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.090
So why am I here today?

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:25.330
Well, I hope you're you'll
understand that the R/pharma is

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:29.440
a very open organization made
up of volunteers we'd welcome

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:31.850
anyone wishing to get
involved, from presenting at

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:35.410
a meeting to reaching out to
the community with a question.

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:37.960
And more on how you can
get involved in 2021

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:39.160
a little later.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.440
But first, I'd like to
share some information

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.285
on how we've evolved since 2018.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:50.110
R/pharma holds a
conference each year.

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:52.960
The first was held
in 2018 at Harvard.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:56.170
And if you're unable to attend,
many of the presentations

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:58.870
are available on the
R/pharma website,

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:02.920
the link is on this slide, and
I invite you to peruse them.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:05.650
We had around 150
attendees, which

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:09.010
was limited by the
size of the venue,

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:10.880
and it ran over two days.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.180
We're lucky to have some
really amazing keynotes

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:14.450
that are listed here.

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:18.070
There are a number of
amazing general presentations

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:23.350
that ranged from RNA sequencing
to how to convert statisticians

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:29.470
to data scientists, to
using R energy analysis to benefit the environment.

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:34.300
One thing was
clear from 2018 was

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:38.840
that Shiny was the main focus
of most presenters' work.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:46.170
In 2019, we repeated to the
conference, this time expanding

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:48.612
to longer workshops
each half a day.

00:04:48.612 --> 00:04:50.070
Once again, the
conference was held

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:52.020
at Harvard, the
mixture of keynotes

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:56.190
and general presentations
for around 150 attendees.

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:58.470
I'd really like to highlight
the workshops here.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:01.950
So we've reached out to a
number of workshop presenters,

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:04.500
all of whom agreed to
put together a half day

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:07.320
workshop at no cost.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:10.230
And all the workshop materials
and many of the presentations

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:13.800
are available through
the R/pharmar website.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:18.450
What was interested in 2019
was still a focus on Shiny,

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:21.610
but there was a huge
focus on reproducibility.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:29.290
So like many other
groups in 2020, early on,

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:32.470
we decided to switch to a
fully virtual conference.

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:38.390
This provided few benefits over
historical in-person meeting.

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:40.700
First, we could
reach more attendees

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:43.730
and we received over
500 registrations

00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:46.110
from 26 countries.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:48.750
We're expecting around
300 and initially, it

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:53.130
prepared for up to 500,
but as soon as they opened,

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:56.790
it was clear that we were
going to hit 1,000 plus.

00:05:56.790 --> 00:06:01.210
And the graph on the right is
actually really interesting.

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:03.600
It shows the registration.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.700
This was registration
without advertising.

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:10.570
So just through word of mouth,
it's amazing how quickly

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:13.200
we had registration.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:15.240
Secondly, we could
attract more presenters

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:17.700
because people didn't
have to travel.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.280
There were more people
willing to present.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:24.360
And in fact, we had over 200
proposals for presentations.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:28.800
Conference was held over three
days each, each four hours long

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:32.130
running in the morning for those
on the West Coast of the US

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:35.230
and the evenings in Europe.

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:38.140
So I also want to highlight
the workshops that

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:41.170
were held for the 2020
conference because

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:43.960
of the virtual nature, we
could organize a little bit

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:47.320
differently, stretched
out over multiple days.

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:50.110
We ran a total of 11
workshops through the week

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:52.570
before the conference,
each 3 to 4 hours

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:55.330
long with some
incredible presenters who

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:57.730
put a lot of time and
effort into building them.

00:06:57.730 --> 00:07:00.550
And all the environments
were pre-configured

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:03.310
under RStudio Cloud.

00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:05.220
So it was very easy
for people to come in

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:08.440
and use the materials and
access them afterwards.

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:13.090
Once again, these were all put
on by the presenters, no charge

00:07:13.090 --> 00:07:16.930
and once again, the
conferences is free anyway.

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:19.540
So it was a really
amazing piece of work.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.750
R/pharma meeting for
2021 is almost certainly

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:28.310
going to be a fully
virtual event.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:31.220
And thinking about the trends
we've seen over the past three

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:34.190
years R is definitely
growing within the pharma

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:36.600
and biotech industries.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.120
Why the rate?

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:41.060
We now have more mature
infrastructures in place

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:45.230
and there's definitely a move
towards more submissions using R.

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:46.850
In addition,
there's greater work

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:50.490
in combining languages such
as R and Python, R and SASS.

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:56.530
I want to switch gears
just a little bit

00:07:56.530 --> 00:07:59.590
and talk about something new.

00:07:59.590 --> 00:08:01.960
We'll be launching a
learning and development

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:06.010
platform this year called
learn r in pharma dot com.

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:09.070
And the goal of this is to help
former professionals develop

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:12.640
the skills needed in the
drug development space.

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:15.460
The R pharma group will be
helping community members put

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:19.780
on workshops, lectures, teaching
labs and Cloud environments

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:22.220
with the aim of building
practical R skills

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:25.600
in a friendly environment.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:28.570
At a very high level,
what we're hoping to do

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:31.690
is to put together a
monthly write up on a topic

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:36.130
and then follow it by a lecture
or workshop or even a workflow,

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:38.320
maybe a community discussion.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:40.870
This is one way that you,
the pharma community,

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:42.100
can contribute.

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:44.680
You'd like to lead a
discussion group, teach a lab,

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:46.060
please get in touch with us.

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:48.850
Contact details will
be on the final slide

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:52.960
and keep an eye out for learn
r in pharma dot com. We'll announce more

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:56.520
through our Twitter feed and
the R/pharma website.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:04.760
The first R/pharma a conference
that we held three years ago

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:07.520
was dominated by
Shiny apps. We can

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:12.200
see how Shiny can play a role
in modernizing the submission

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:15.620
process, but it's
fraught with challenges.

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:19.040
These can be related
to technology,

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:24.620
regulatory agencies, training
and validation, qualification,

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:29.710
and even people and
culture, plus others.

00:09:29.710 --> 00:09:34.090
In 2021, R/pharma
wants to tackle Shiny

00:09:34.090 --> 00:09:37.360
for submissions as part of
learn r in pharma dot com.

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:44.950
R/pharma is all about
the community helping

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:47.140
the community. This
year, we're looking

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:51.423
to launch learn r in pharma
dot com, which I spoke about.

00:09:51.423 --> 00:09:52.840
So if you have an
idea for a write

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:56.110
up, followed by a lecture,
workshop, discussion,

00:09:56.110 --> 00:10:00.140
please reach out through the
website, email, or Twitter,

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:02.560
and we can help you
find like minded people,

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:04.360
provide logistics.

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:09.730
Some examples might be combining
R and Python or building a

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:12.290
validation framework.

00:10:12.290 --> 00:10:15.680
Shiny for submissions
will be part of this, too.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:19.310
Finally, a huge thank you to all
of the presenters and workshop

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:21.300
hosts over the past
three years, putting

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:23.450
So much effort, along
with the advisory groups

00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:26.180
and Harvard who have
supported the conferences

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:30.070
that we've held, and I thank
you for your attention.

